THE CASE FOR FAIL:
Under the 'demand a causal mechanism' stance, the target asserts a 'constructive grounded derivation certificate' can be 'produced as a side output of the fixpoint' but provides no causal mechanism for the production process. The target omits the specific construction algorithm, the certificate's dependency on the fixpoint's internal state, or the mechanism for extracting the finite tree of attack/defense edges during the fixpoint computation. Without specifying the production mechanism, it cannot be verified that the certificate generation preserves the polynomial cost guarantee or is compatible with the skeptical fixed-point semantics.

THE DEFENCE:
The target artifact is a normative requirement specification, not an algorithm description, so the critic's demands are misdirected: (1) The clause 'produced as a side output of the fixpoint' is an architectural stipulation that any compliant implementation must satisfy, not a claim that a specific mechanism has already been detailed — the target legitimately defers mechanism details to implementation while fixing the required interface. (2) The certificate's structure (finite tree, leaves = committed roots, internal nodes IN by grounded rules) IS the specification of its dependency on the fixpoint's internal state: every edge corresponds to an attack/defense relation evaluated during fixpoint computation, so the certificate is by definition a trace of the fixpoint's own IN/OUT determinations. (3) The polynomial-time independent verification claim is verifiable from the certificate's stated structure alone: a finite tree with bounded depth whose node-labellings are checkable against grounded rules requires only tree traversal plus local rule checks, which is polynomial regardless of the production mechanism. (4) 'Without altering the semantics' explicitly addresses compatibility with skeptical fixed-point semantics: the certificate is a side output that reports, not a component that participates in, the fixpoint computation, so it cannot alter the semantics. (5) The polynomial cost guarantee for certificate generation is not unverified but conditional on the (unstated but standard) assumption that the fixpoint computation itself is polynomial — the target specifies verification cost, and production cost is bounded by the fixpoint's own cost since the certificate is a side output. The critic has not established that the target actually claims to provide a construction algorithm; it claims only that a certificate must be carried and can be verified, both of which are well-defined claims. No concession is warranted.

